# hakai — suggested Hyprland keybinds.
#
# Not applied automatically by the package (editing a user's own dotfiles from a package
# install is the kind of thing that should never happen silently) — copy whatever you want
# into your own config instead.
#
# SUPER SHIFT H is what this project itself uses — chosen after SUPER SHIFT D, Omarchy's
# own default keybind (matching its usual "SUPER SHIFT + first letter" app-launcher
# pattern) turned out to already be bound to LazyDocker on a real Omarchy install. Check
# `omarchy menu keybindings --print` (or `Super K`) before relying on H being free on your
# own machine too, since Omarchy's default bindings have changed across its major
# versions — as has the config format itself (see below).
#
# WHICH FORM YOU NEED DEPENDS ON YOUR HYPRLAND VERSION.
#
# Hyprland 0.55+ moved to a Lua config (confirmed the hard way — a plain `hyprctl keyword`
# call, and appending the old hyprlang form below to `~/.config/hypr/bindings.conf`, both
# silently did nothing on a real, current Omarchy install; Omarchy's own convenience
# wrapper is `o.bind(keys, description, command)`, not raw Hyprland `hl.bind`). Add these
# two lines to ~/.config/hypr/bindings.lua (the file Omarchy's own hyprland.lua actually
# `require()`s — not bindings.conf, which nothing loads once you're on Lua), then
# `hyprctl reload`:
#
#   o.bind("SUPER + SHIFT + H", "Destroy the desktop", "uwsm app -- hakai")
#   o.bind("SUPER + SHIFT + ALT + H", "Stop destroying it", "pkill -x hakai")
#
# On an older, pre-Lua Omarchy install, the classic hyprlang form goes in
# ~/.config/hypr/bindings.conf instead:
#
bindd = SUPER SHIFT, H, Destroy the desktop, exec, uwsm app -- hakai
bindd = SUPER SHIFT ALT, H, Stop destroying it, exec, pkill -x hakai
